I have to say, Tom, his biographers, and especially if they're not American and not as keen on him as you are.

615
00:50:37,660 --> 00:50:39,180
So one biography calls him...

616
00:50:39,180 --> 00:50:42,340
I wouldn't say I'm keen, but I like his dash and his swag.

617
00:50:42,380 --> 00:50:46,600
One biography calls him vain, naive, immature, and egocentric.

618
00:50:46,800 --> 00:50:47,720
And my favorite one...

619
00:50:47,720 --> 00:50:48,200
What's not to like?

620
00:50:48,300 --> 00:50:52,160
It's from the Histoire et Dictionnaire de la Révolution Française,

621
00:50:52,620 --> 00:50:57,000
which was compiled by French scholars in the 1980s for the bicentenary.

622
00:50:57,340 --> 00:51:00,220
And the entry of Lafayette describes him...

623
00:51:00,240 --> 00:51:02,800
...as an empty-headed political dwarf.

624
00:51:03,620 --> 00:51:04,400
Yeah, brilliant.

625
00:51:05,220 --> 00:51:09,220
Yeah, I mean, I'm not saying he's a political genius, but he has swagger and dash.

626
00:51:09,400 --> 00:51:10,060
Yeah, and you love that.

627
00:51:10,080 --> 00:51:10,760
You love that.

628
00:51:10,840 --> 00:51:12,260
You love a political dwarf.

629
00:51:13,860 --> 00:51:15,000
If he dresses well.

630
00:51:15,260 --> 00:51:17,640
So they arrive at the Hotel de Ville.

631
00:51:19,500 --> 00:51:21,500
Huge cheers, all this kind of stuff.

632
00:51:22,380 --> 00:51:25,580
Bailly, the astronomer, people say, you be the mayor of Paris now.

633
00:51:25,660 --> 00:51:26,740
So he's going to be the mayor of Paris.

634
00:51:27,540 --> 00:51:30,220
And Lafayette accepts the commandment.

635
00:51:30,240 --> 00:51:34,560
He accepts the command of this new militia, the bourgeois guard, the National Guard, as it becomes.

636
00:51:35,640 --> 00:51:40,340
And it's Lafayette who says, why don't we, as a mark of compromise,

637
00:51:41,340 --> 00:51:44,360
and the coming together of all these different forces,

638
00:51:44,680 --> 00:51:50,420
we will add to the red and blue colors of Paris the white of the Bourbon dynasty.

639
00:51:50,740 --> 00:51:52,420
So, Dominic, I put it to you.

640
00:51:52,440 --> 00:51:55,080
Is this the action of an empty-headed political dwarf?

641
00:51:55,520 --> 00:51:56,420
Yeah, it's a terrible fact.

642
00:51:56,420 --> 00:52:00,020
Or a man with an eye for political symbolism that has endured to the present day?

643
00:52:00,060 --> 00:52:00,200
Well, yeah.

644
00:52:00,200 --> 00:52:03,080
I mean, it depends what your views on the French flag, Tom.

645
00:52:03,440 --> 00:52:10,320
Do you like the old flag, the flag of tradition, honor, and the age-old customs?

646
00:52:10,340 --> 00:52:11,720
I have a lot of time for a tricolour.

647
00:52:11,720 --> 00:52:12,320
Of a proud people?

648
00:52:12,620 --> 00:52:14,960
Or do you like a boring tricolour flag?

649
00:52:15,120 --> 00:52:16,800
I don't rate a tricolour flag, personally.

650
00:52:16,840 --> 00:52:17,520
You know my methods.

651
00:52:17,880 --> 00:52:20,840
Anyway, so yeah, this is the origins of the French flag.

652
00:52:21,040 --> 00:52:24,960
The next day, the 16th, so only two days after the fall of the Bastille,

653
00:52:25,060 --> 00:52:27,700
the king holds his final ever royal council.

654
00:52:27,860 --> 00:52:30,180
His brothers and his...

655
00:52:30,200 --> 00:52:35,420
His relatives, a lot of them have basically now given up on the whole process.

656
00:52:36,780 --> 00:52:38,960
Again, they say to him, you should leave.

657
00:52:39,060 --> 00:52:40,100
You should get to the frontier.

658
00:52:40,340 --> 00:52:41,320
Get loyal troops.

659
00:52:41,540 --> 00:52:42,540
This is all out of control.

660
00:52:43,200 --> 00:52:47,900
But actually, some people say, now we're not even sure we can get there in safety.

661
00:52:48,040 --> 00:52:49,360
That safety cannot be guaranteed.

662
00:52:49,360 --> 00:52:53,480
We just have to hope somehow, stick this out, and hope we can turn this around.

663
00:52:54,480 --> 00:52:55,800
But Dominic, you know who does go?

664
00:52:56,080 --> 00:52:56,600
Tell me.

665
00:52:56,840 --> 00:52:58,300
The Duchess de Polignac.

666
00:52:58,380 --> 00:52:59,520
Yeah, Marianne Trenet's friend.

667
00:52:59,720 --> 00:53:00,180
Marianne Trenet.

668
00:53:00,200 --> 00:53:00,840
Marianne Trenet's great friend.

669
00:53:00,940 --> 00:53:01,080
Yeah.

670
00:53:01,160 --> 00:53:04,640
And who doesn't go is our other great friend, the Princess de Lamballe.

671
00:53:04,680 --> 00:53:04,900
I know.

672
00:53:05,020 --> 00:53:06,500
Should have gone, Tom.

673
00:53:06,700 --> 00:53:07,540
Princess de Lamballe.

674
00:53:07,720 --> 00:53:08,360
As events will prove.

675
00:53:08,360 --> 00:53:09,040
As events will prove.

676
00:53:09,120 --> 00:53:10,700
So quite a lot of them do go that night.

677
00:53:10,800 --> 00:53:14,960
The Comte d'Artois, the Prince de Conti, the Prince de Condé.

678
00:53:15,400 --> 00:53:17,360
So these are the first émigré.

679
00:53:17,740 --> 00:53:22,080
So for the first time, you have people now fleeing France for the frontiers,

680
00:53:23,860 --> 00:53:27,560
determined that there will be no compromise with the revolution.

681
00:53:27,780 --> 00:53:29,100
But Louis himself does not flee.

682
00:53:30,360 --> 00:53:35,580
He, instead, the following day, the 17th, makes an extraordinary gesture.

683
00:53:36,080 --> 00:53:40,820
He decides to go to Paris himself in an undecorated carriage,

684
00:53:41,140 --> 00:53:43,180
wearing a plain, undecorated coat.

685
00:53:43,500 --> 00:53:49,240
And he is greeted at the gates of Paris by the new mayor, Bailly,

686
00:53:49,420 --> 00:53:51,720
the astronomer from the National Assembly.

687
00:53:51,960 --> 00:53:54,180
And Bailly hands him the keys of the city.

688
00:53:54,740 --> 00:53:59,120
And he says, these are the same keys that were presented to Henri IV,

689
00:53:59,180 --> 00:53:59,840
Henry IV.

690
00:54:00,520 --> 00:54:04,780
A great folk hero to the French people, the king who had abandoned Protestantism

691
00:54:05,880 --> 00:54:08,560
because he said, you know, France, what is it?

692
00:54:08,600 --> 00:54:10,640
France, Paris is worth a mass, is it?

693
00:54:10,680 --> 00:54:11,320
Paris is worth a mass.

694
00:54:11,320 --> 00:54:12,140
Paris is worth a mass, yeah.

695
00:54:12,200 --> 00:54:15,700
Also, he's very keen that every peasant should have a chicken in their head.

696
00:54:15,760 --> 00:54:16,080
Exactly.

697
00:54:16,280 --> 00:54:17,120
Everybody loves him.

698
00:54:17,320 --> 00:54:18,560
Chickens and Catholicism.

699
00:54:19,520 --> 00:54:24,060
And Bailly says to Louis, these keys were given to Henry IV.

700
00:54:24,300 --> 00:54:29,080
He had conquered his people, but now it is his people who have conquered their king.

701
00:54:29,380 --> 00:54:30,180
I mean, who wants to hear?

702
00:54:30,200 --> 00:54:30,300
Who wants to hear that?

703
00:54:31,200 --> 00:54:33,240
Poor Louis, like, smiling weakly.

704
00:54:35,340 --> 00:54:39,820
Anyway, Lafayette is there with his new cockade, the new colors, red, white, and blue.

705
00:54:39,880 --> 00:54:40,200
I'm sure.

706
00:54:40,920 --> 00:54:44,340
There are loads of National Guards now lining the streets.

707
00:54:44,900 --> 00:54:46,640
They process through the streets.

708
00:54:46,840 --> 00:54:50,940
Market women dressed in white, making up the rear of the procession.

709
00:54:50,940 --> 00:54:53,680
We'll be hearing more about market women, I imagine, in the next couple of days.

710
00:54:53,840 --> 00:54:54,320
We will, yeah.

711
00:54:54,920 --> 00:54:59,740
And people are chanting, not long live the king, but long live the nation.

712
00:54:59,780 --> 00:55:00,180
Long live the nation.

713
00:55:01,220 --> 00:55:04,240
They get to the city hall, and there there's a great banner.

714
00:55:04,860 --> 00:55:09,020
Louis XVI, father of the French, the king of a free people.

715
00:55:09,540 --> 00:55:12,920
And Dominic Key there, he's not described as the king of France.

716
00:55:12,940 --> 00:55:13,780
No, but of the French.

717
00:55:13,820 --> 00:55:17,240
So the idea that France is the property of the king is gone.

718
00:55:17,280 --> 00:55:17,660
Exactly.

719
00:55:18,480 --> 00:55:21,260
Louis gets onto a throne, and people make speeches.

720
00:55:21,680 --> 00:55:25,100
And he's said to have listened with tears running down his cheeks.

721
00:55:25,160 --> 00:55:27,020
Of course, some people say, wonderful.

722
00:55:27,220 --> 00:55:28,560
Tears of patriotic pride.

723
00:55:29,040 --> 00:55:29,780
Others say.

724
00:55:30,200 --> 00:55:31,100
He's gutted.

725
00:55:31,140 --> 00:55:32,900
This is like the worst day of his life.

726
00:55:34,040 --> 00:55:37,680
Bailly gives him a tricolour cockade, red, white, and blue.

727
00:55:37,760 --> 00:55:39,960
And the king sticks it in his hat, and everybody cheers.

728
00:55:40,700 --> 00:55:42,760
But what he is thinking, we don't know.

729
00:55:42,840 --> 00:55:45,900
We know he got back to Versailles that night about 10 o'clock.

730
00:55:46,020 --> 00:55:49,620
And Marianne Swinette and his family were so worried about him.

731
00:55:50,100 --> 00:55:54,420
But what he said to them, what he made of it all, remains a mystery.

732
00:55:55,340 --> 00:55:59,680
Most people, though, at the time, thought this was a truly exceptional,

733
00:56:00,200 --> 00:56:01,440
truly remarkable event in history.

734
00:56:01,800 --> 00:56:06,600
One third estate deputy, a guy called Jean-Antoine Huguet, said it was unique

735
00:56:06,600 --> 00:56:10,000
in the annals of the universe to see a people that had taken up arms against

736
00:56:10,000 --> 00:56:15,100
their king now reunited with their king in a world in which the balance

737
00:56:15,100 --> 00:56:16,300
of power completely changed.

738
00:56:16,560 --> 00:56:23,080
He says, it was reserved for the French nation to give this example to the universe.

739
00:56:23,580 --> 00:56:27,660
And that universalism, we'll be talking about this tomorrow when you do.

740
00:56:27,680 --> 00:56:27,960
We will.

741
00:56:27,960 --> 00:56:33,500
When you get onto the sort of ideological shift that the French Revolution represents,

742
00:56:33,920 --> 00:56:37,500
that universalism is so key to this whole story.

743
00:56:38,140 --> 00:56:42,260
There's a great source for this whole period, the letters of a bookseller called Nicolas

744
00:56:42,260 --> 00:56:42,760
Rouault.

745
00:56:43,200 --> 00:56:45,000
And he wrote to his brother after this.

746
00:56:45,060 --> 00:56:46,400
He said, everything will now change.

747
00:56:46,780 --> 00:56:49,640
Morals, opinions, laws, customs, usages, government.

748
00:56:49,960 --> 00:56:53,620
In very little time, we will be new men.

749
00:56:53,840 --> 00:56:56,260
And that, of course, is the French Revolution's appeal.

750
00:56:56,420 --> 00:56:57,800
The idea of starting anew.

751
00:56:57,960 --> 00:57:00,360
And making, you know, a better world.

752
00:57:00,780 --> 00:57:05,300
But actually, Tom, it would be, to be Burkean, I think it would be wrong

753
00:57:05,300 --> 00:57:10,720
to end on the note of optimism because actually the violence doesn't

754
00:57:10,720 --> 00:57:12,640
end with the fall of the Bastille.

755
00:57:12,700 --> 00:57:14,780
Something has been unleashed now, I would say.

756
00:57:15,000 --> 00:57:19,000
And I know this is just my own prejudice talking, but I would say something has

757
00:57:19,000 --> 00:57:21,980
been unleashed that will, cannot now be put back in its box.

758
00:57:22,300 --> 00:57:24,560
And the example of this is what we began with.

759
00:57:24,700 --> 00:57:27,940
So seven days, one week after the fall of the Bastille.

760
00:57:29,720 --> 00:57:36,200
The food prices are still very high, and there are rumours that an official, a royal official

761
00:57:36,200 --> 00:57:43,120
in the war ministry called Joseph Foulon de Douai had said about the poor, let them eat straw.

762
00:57:43,740 --> 00:57:46,120
Whether he did say it, we do not know.

763
00:57:47,280 --> 00:57:48,960
A crowd grabbed him.

764
00:57:49,440 --> 00:57:51,000
They, they hanged him.

765
00:57:51,540 --> 00:57:52,640
They cut off his head.

766
00:57:53,060 --> 00:57:57,820
They stuffed grass and straw in his mouth, and they paraded his head around the city on a

767
00:57:57,820 --> 00:57:57,980
pike.

768
00:57:58,580 --> 00:58:02,920
And then they saw his son-in-law, who was the intendant of Paris, the royal official

769
00:58:03,410 --> 00:58:07,460
responsible for Paris, who was Berthier de Sauvigny, the man you began with.

770
00:58:08,060 --> 00:58:12,620
And they dragged, they grabbed, they grabbed him and they forced him to accompany them

771
00:58:12,620 --> 00:58:13,340
round the streets.

772
00:58:13,620 --> 00:58:18,440
They held the head of Foulon in front of him, his father-in-law, and they chanted at

773
00:58:18,440 --> 00:58:20,760
him, kiss papa, kiss papa.

774
00:58:21,000 --> 00:58:24,920
I mean, an absolutely, unbelievably horrendous scene.

775
00:58:25,400 --> 00:58:26,760
They got to the city hall.

776
00:58:26,880 --> 00:58:27,800
They killed him.

777
00:58:27,820 --> 00:58:32,980
They tore, they literally tore the heart out of his body and threw it at the walls of the

778
00:58:32,980 --> 00:58:33,940
city hall.

779
00:58:34,240 --> 00:58:38,340
And then they put his head on a pike too, alongside his father-in-law's and paraded

780
00:58:38,340 --> 00:58:38,720
it around.

781
00:58:39,480 --> 00:58:44,640
And that newspaper you mentioned, Élysée Lustelot's Révolution de Paris, he wrote

782
00:58:44,640 --> 00:58:45,740
a big article about this.

783
00:58:45,780 --> 00:58:46,560
That's what we quoted.

784
00:58:47,160 --> 00:58:49,240
And Lustelot said, it's fine.

785
00:58:49,500 --> 00:58:52,460
They were both traitors to the nation, traitors to the people.

786
00:58:53,360 --> 00:58:57,020
And actually, it's only by killing such people that Frenchmen,

787
00:58:57,020 --> 00:58:58,880
you will be free.

788
00:58:59,440 --> 00:59:04,140
And as Simon Sharma says in his book, Citizens, this isn't the end of something.

789
00:59:04,340 --> 00:59:05,740
This is just the beginning.

790
00:59:05,920 --> 00:59:10,140
When he's described these events, it's then that Sharma writes, violence was not just

791
00:59:10,140 --> 00:59:12,460
an unfortunate side effect of the French Revolution.

792
00:59:12,520 --> 00:59:15,080
It was the revolution's source of collective energy.

793
00:59:15,380 --> 00:59:19,580
It was violence that made the revolution revolutionary.

794
00:59:20,220 --> 00:59:23,900
And I'm afraid there will be a lot more violence to come.

795
00:59:24,020 --> 00:59:26,800
But I think worth mentioning.

796
00:59:26,800 --> 00:59:32,600
As well, there will also be a lot more idealism and abstract nouns to come.

797
00:59:32,640 --> 00:59:33,360
So we will see you then.

798
00:59:33,400 --> 00:59:33,480
Brilliant.

799
00:59:33,560 --> 00:59:33,800
Bye-bye.

800
00:59:33,820 --> 00:59:34,040
Bye-bye.

